<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as density from './statements/density.md';
	import * as information from './statements/information.md';
	import * as tangentDensity from './statements/tangent-density.md';
	import * as wrapping from './statements/wrapping.md';
	import * as invariant from './statements/invariant.md';
</script>

## The problem

[The core](../core/math.svx) can build, query and differentiate a model, but it has no
distribution: a term's log-density is whatever composite its author writes, and its tests keep a
private normal of a real quantity for want of a public one. The normal distribution is the one
nearly every model skoro is meant for reaches first. A prior on a state, a motion model, a
measurement with Gaussian noise, and the replacement a retirement leaves behind under a Laplace
approximation are all normal. It is therefore the first distribution to return from the `archive`
branch.

The archive's normal was written for any manifold: its term was the Gaussian log-density of the
tangent $\point \ominus \mu$ at the mean, whitened by a triangular factor of the covariance. That
form is right on a flat space and on none other without a correction. The archive had no positive
type, but carried over unchanged to the core's positive orthant, the form would drop the Jacobian
of the logarithmic chart, so that its density would not be with respect to the base measure the
core fixes for positive quantities ([](eq:archive/core/math/log-chart-density)), and its sampler,
drawing exactly from the normal in the chart, would not be exact for its own term. On a curved type
such as the rotations it omitted the volume factor of the retraction, and its expected information
held only to second order in the dispersion. This document works out the
normal on exactly the spaces where it can be stated with respect to the core's base measures
without approximation, and the parts of it that the rest of skoro will need: how its scale is
given, how it is drawn, its expected information, and its use as a likelihood factor over values
that are not quantities.

The archive's form was nonetheless the one a curved space needs. A measurement of a rotation, of a
direction or of a pose is most cleanly modelled as what was measured, the measured quantity moved
by normal noise, $h(\point) \oplus \varepsilon$, rather than as the residual of a normal, and a
model that holds the measurement itself can observe it in one query and simulate it in another. The
archive's fault was its density, which omitted the volume of the chart at the mean. With that
volume, which [the note on densities in tangent charts](../../math/chart-volume/) works out for
the rotations, the rigid motions and the sphere, the same form has an exact density on every space
whose chart is global, and one exact to within a stated tail on the exponential charts of the
groups. This document therefore also works out the *tangent normal*, the law of $\mu \oplus
\varepsilon$ on any space whose chart's volume is known, of which the normal is the case of the
flat spaces, and what a space must supply to carry it.

## Setting

A *flat-in-disguise* space, in this document, is a space of the core whose inverse retraction at
any one point is a chart of the whole space: the real line, vectors $\R^n$ and matrices
$\R^{m \times n}$, read as vectors column by column, the positive orthant $\positive^n$ with its
retraction [](eq:archive/core/math/positive), and products of these, with the stacked tangent
[](eq:math/product-manifold/stack). For such a space $\manifold$, of dimension $\dimension$, and
any $\mu \in \manifold$, the map $\point \mapsto \point \ominus \mu$ is a diffeomorphism of
$\manifold$ onto $\R^\dimension$ whose inverse is $\tangent \mapsto \mu \oplus \tangent$, and both
identities

$$
	(\point \oplus \tangent) \ominus \mu = (\point \ominus \mu) + \tangent,
	\qquad
	\point \ominus (\mu \oplus u) = (\point \ominus \mu) - u @tag(translation)
$$

hold exactly, for every $\tangent$ and $u$: on a Euclidean factor because $\ominus$ is subtraction,
and on a positive one because it is subtraction of logarithms. Each such space is an open subset of
$\R^\dimension$ in its storage coordinates, its dimension equals its storage size, and its base
measure is Lebesgue measure there ([the core's appendix](../core/math.svx#appendix-the-probability-space-of-a-model)).
Write $P$ for the storage coordinates that belong to positive factors, and

$$
	J(\point) = \sum_{i \in P} \log \point_i , @tag(jacobian)
$$

which is zero on a space with no positive factor.

The normal's *mean* is $\mu \in \manifold$, and its *scale* is a symmetric positive definite
$\dimension \times \dimension$ matrix, given in one of the forms of [The scale](#the-scale) and
read, in every one of them, through its *whitening* $W$: the lower-triangular matrix with a
positive diagonal such that the precision $\Lambda = \Sigma^{-1}$ is $W\transpose W$. Then
$L = W^{-1}$ is lower triangular with a positive diagonal and $\Sigma = L L\transpose$. Both are
unique: $L$ is the Cholesky factor of $\Sigma$.

## The density

The normal $\mathcal N(\mu, \Sigma)$ on a flat-in-disguise space $\manifold$ is the law of
$\mu \oplus \varepsilon$, where $\varepsilon$ is normal on $\R^\dimension$ with mean zero and
covariance $\Sigma$. Its term is

$$
	\logterm_{\mathcal N}(\point;\, \mu, W)
	= -\tfrac12 \bigl\lVert W (\point \ominus \mu) \bigr\rVert^2
	+ \sum_{i=1}^{\dimension} \log W_{ii}
	- \tfrac{\dimension}{2} \log 2\pi
	- J(\point), @tag(term)
$$

and $r = W (\point \ominus \mu)$ is its *whitened residual*, a standard normal vector when
$\randomPoint$ is drawn from the normal.

<Statement {...density} />

<Proof>

The map $\tangent \mapsto \mu \oplus \tangent$ is a diffeomorphism of $\R^\dimension$ onto
$\manifold$, with inverse $\point \mapsto \point \ominus \mu$. The vector $\varepsilon = L \zeta$ is
normal with mean zero and covariance $L L\transpose = \Sigma$, so its density at $\tangent$ is
$(2\pi)^{-\dimension/2} \det W \exp(-\tfrac12 \lVert W \tangent \rVert^2)$, since
$(\det \Sigma)^{-1/2} = \det W$ and $\tangent\transpose \Sigma^{-1} \tangent = \lVert W \tangent
\rVert^2$. By the change of variables $\tangent = \point \ominus \mu$, the
density of $\randomPoint = \mu \oplus \varepsilon$ with respect to Lebesgue measure on $\manifold$
is that density at $\point \ominus \mu$ times $\lvert \det \partial (\point \ominus \mu) / \partial
\point \rvert$, the derivative taken in storage coordinates. The inverse retraction acts factor by
factor, so that derivative is block diagonal: the identity on a Euclidean factor, and
$\operatorname{diag}(1 / \point)$ on a positive one, where $\point \ominus \mu = \log \point - \log
\mu$. Its determinant is $\prod_{i \in P} \point_i^{-1}$, whose logarithm is $-J(\point)$.

</Proof>

The Jacobian $J$ is what keeps the term's base measure the core's. It does not depend on $\mu$ or
on the scale, so it changes no derivative with respect to them, but it is a function of $\point$
and it is not a constant of a query that leaves $\point$ free: since $J(\point \oplus \tangent) =
J(\point) + \sum_{i \in P} \tangent_i$, the term's $-J$ adds $-1$ to its chart gradient at each
positive coordinate. On a positive factor it moves the mode of the density away from $\mu$, as it
must, because the density is with respect to Lebesgue measure in storage coordinates and not in the
chart. Without it, three things the core
relies on would fail: the sampler below would not be exact for the term, so the importance weight
[](eq:archive/core/math/weight) would be wrong by $J$ at every draw; a quadrature of the term over
$\point$, as in the core's tests of retirement, would not integrate to one; and a model mixing the
normal with another distribution of a positive quantity would compare densities with respect to
two different measures.

On a positive factor the normal is the log-normal distribution: $\log \randomPoint$ is normal with
mean $\log \mu$ and covariance $\Sigma$, so $\mu$ is the median of each coordinate rather than its
mean, which is $\mu_i e^{\Sigma_{ii} / 2}$. On a Euclidean factor it is the familiar normal, with
mean $\mu$ and covariance $\Sigma$. On a product the two kinds of factor are correlated through
$\Sigma$ in their tangent coordinates, which is what a state with a position and a positive scale
needs.

**Curved types.** On an embedded type, such as the sphere of the core's acceptance test or the
rotations of a later entry, the chart at $\mu$ does not satisfy [](eq:translation), may not be a
diffeomorphism of $\R^\dimension$ onto the type (on the sphere it misses a point, on the rotations
it may wrap around), and the density of the pushed-forward normal carries the volume of that chart
in place of $J$. That density is the tangent normal's, in [The tangent normal](#the-tangent-normal),
of which the normal of this section is the case of the flat-in-disguise spaces.

## The scale

The term [](eq:term) reads the scale through $W$ alone, and $W$ enters linearly in the residual
and through the logarithms of its diagonal in the normalizer. A model gives the scale in whichever
form it has, and each form is a map to $W$ composed from the core's primitives, so that its
derivatives, of both orders, follow by composition:

| form | given | $W$ | $\sum_i \log W_{ii}$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| isotropic | $\sigma \in \positive$, with $\Sigma = \sigma^2 I$ | $\sigma^{-1} I$ | $-\dimension \log \sigma$ |
| diagonal | $\sigma \in \positive^\dimension$, with $\Sigma = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma)^2$ | $\operatorname{diag}(\sigma)^{-1}$ | $-\sum_i \log \sigma_i$ |
| covariance factor | $L$, with $\Sigma = L L\transpose$ | $L^{-1}$ | $-\sum_i \log L_{ii}$ |
| whitening | $W$, with $\Lambda = W\transpose W$ | $W$ | $\sum_i \log W_{ii}$ |

The first two are the forms most models write, a measurement's standard deviation or a motion
model's per-axis noise, and their whitened residual is the elementwise product of $\point \ominus
\mu$ with $\sigma^{-1}$, which the core composes as the reciprocal of a positive value read as a
real. The whitening is the form a precision arrives in: elimination produces information rather
than covariance, and a retirement's replacement is written in it (see
[As a likelihood factor](#as-a-likelihood-factor)). The residual is then the product $W (\point
\ominus \mu)$, and nothing is inverted. The covariance factor is the form a user with a full
covariance writes, and its residual is the solution $z$ of $L z = \point \ominus \mu$, by forward
substitution,

$$
	z_i = \frac{1}{L_{ii}} \Bigl( (\point \ominus \mu)_i - \sum_{j < i} L_{ij}\, z_j \Bigr),
	\qquad i = 1, \dots, \dimension, @tag(forward-substitution)
$$

a composite of $\dimension$ steps, each a sum of products and a multiplication by the reciprocal
of a positive diagonal entry. When $L$ is fixed in a query, $W = L^{-1}$ can instead be computed
once, column by column by the same substitution against the columns of the identity, and folded,
as the core folds every value reached only from fixed inputs; when $L$ is free, the residual is
solved on every evaluation, and its derivatives with respect to $L$ come with it.

**All four forms.** The normal offers all four, because each is the form some model has, and none
is reached from another without a computation the model did not ask for. The isotropic and
diagonal forms need no linear algebra at all. The whitening is what elimination and a retirement's
replacement produce, and inverting it to a covariance would cost a solve and lose the sparsity
elimination keeps. The covariance factor is what a user with a full covariance, from a datasheet
or a calibration, writes; it costs the substitution [](eq:forward-substitution), and a solve on
every evaluation when it is free, which is the price of not inverting a matrix the user has and
the term does not need inverted. Every form is a map to the same $W$, so the rest of this document,
the tangent normal included, holds for each of them alike.

**A triangular factor as a quantity.** When a scale is to be estimated, its factor is a quantity,
and its type must have exactly as many tangent directions as the factor has free entries,
$\dimension (\dimension + 1) / 2$. A matrix $\R^{\dimension \times \dimension}$ would have
$\dimension (\dimension - 1) / 2$ more, along which the term does not change: the chart Hessian and
the expected information would be singular in them, and an estimator would have to know to ignore
them. The core already has the right type, a product
$\positive^\dimension \times \R^{\dimension (\dimension - 1) / 2}$ of the diagonal and the entries
strictly below it, in a fixed order. Its positive factor keeps the diagonal positive, so that the
factor is the unique Cholesky factor, its logarithms are defined, and no absolute value is needed,
which the core does not have. Its chart on the diagonal is logarithmic, so a tangent coordinate of
the diagonal is a relative change: these are the log-Cholesky coordinates of a covariance. For
$\dimension = 1$ the strictly lower part is empty and the factor is a positive scalar, the
isotropic form. The same type serves a covariance factor and a whitening, and a positive vector
serves a diagonal scale.

## The sampler

The normal draws $\point = \mu \oplus L \zeta$, with $\zeta$ standard normal on
$\R^\dimension$. By [%full](statement:density), that draw has the density $\exp
\logterm_{\mathcal N}$, Jacobian included, so the sampler is exact for the term
[](eq:term) and contributes nothing to the importance weight. The coordinates of $\zeta$ are drawn
from the quantity's key as the core draws every variate (charter, position 10): $\dimension$
uniform variates $u_i \in (0, 1)$, in double precision on the host, each turned into
$\zeta_i = \Phi^{-1}(u_i)$ by the standard normal quantile. A draw is then a deterministic
function of the key, smooth in $\mu$ and the scale, and the same key gives the same draw at either
precision, up to the rounding of the final cast.

Two computations in a draw are approximate, and neither is a matter of the model. The quantile
$\Phi^{-1}$ has no closed form; Wichura's algorithm AS 241, which the core's tests already use, is
a rational approximation in three pieces accurate to about $10^{-16}$ relative error, below the
rounding of double precision. And $L \zeta$ needs $L$, which the whitening form must invert: it is
the solution $y$ of $W y = \zeta$, found on the host, in double precision, by the substitution
[](eq:forward-substitution) with $W$ in place of $L$, as the charter has every solve run
(position 11). Inversion of the distribution function was chosen over
the Box–Muller transform because it spends exactly one uniform variate per coordinate, so that the
draw of coordinate $i$ depends on $u_i$ alone, and is monotone in it.

## The expected information

An estimator that uses the expected information of the model in place of its Hessian, Fisher
scoring or a Riemannian sampler, asks of each term a matrix that is positive semidefinite by
construction. For the normal it has a closed form, and on flat-in-disguise spaces that form is
exact.

<Statement {...information} />

<Proof>

By [](eq:translation), along $\point \oplus t \tangent$ and $\mu \oplus t u$ the tangent
$\varepsilon = \point \ominus \mu$ moves to $\varepsilon + t(\tangent - u)$ exactly, and $J$ moves
linearly, so the chart Hessian of [](eq:term) in $(\point, \mu)$ is that of
$\tfrac12 \varepsilon\transpose \Lambda \varepsilon$ in $\varepsilon$ composed with
$(\tangent, u) \mapsto \tangent - u$: the upper-left blocks of $\mathcal I$, at every point, before
any expectation. The mixed second derivative in $(\varepsilon, \theta_i)$ is
$\partial_i \Lambda\, \varepsilon$, whose expectation is zero since $\varepsilon$ has mean zero. In
$\theta$, the negative term is $\tfrac12 \varepsilon\transpose \Lambda \varepsilon - \tfrac12 \log
\det \Lambda$ plus what does not depend on $\theta$, since $\sum_i \log W_{ii} = \frac12 \log \det
\Lambda$. With $\partial_j \log \det \Lambda = \operatorname{tr}(\Sigma\, \partial_j \Lambda)$ and
$\partial_i \Sigma = -\Sigma\, \partial_i \Lambda\, \Sigma$, and $\E[\varepsilon
\varepsilon\transpose] = \Sigma$,

$$
	\E\Bigl[\partial_i \partial_j \bigl(\tfrac12 \varepsilon\transpose \Lambda \varepsilon - \tfrac12 \log \det \Lambda\bigr)\Bigr]
	= \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}(\partial_i \partial_j \Lambda\, \Sigma)
	- \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}(\partial_i \Sigma\, \partial_j \Lambda)
	- \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}(\Sigma\, \partial_i \partial_j \Lambda)
	= \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}(\Sigma\, \partial_i \Lambda\, \Sigma\, \partial_j \Lambda) .
$$

Neither the mean nor $J$ depends on $\theta$, so the score in $\theta$ is that of a centred normal,
and the expected negative Hessian equals its covariance, the Fisher information.

</Proof>

The archive's information was a matrix of the same blocks, but with $\pm I$ in place of the
derivatives of $\point \ominus \mu$, which on a group hold only at $\point = \mu$; it was therefore
correct to second order in the dispersion there. Here [](eq:translation) makes those derivatives
exactly $\pm I$, and nothing is approximated.

**A root.** An estimator assembles the information of a model from roots of its terms', matrices
$C$ with $C\transpose C = \mathcal I$, composed with the Jacobians of the term's inputs with
respect to the free quantities. With $T_i = L\transpose\, \partial_i \Lambda\, L$, symmetric, the
trace is $\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma\, \partial_i \Lambda\, \Sigma\, \partial_j \Lambda) =
\operatorname{tr}(T_i T_j) = \sum_a (T_i)_{aa} (T_j)_{aa} + 2 \sum_{a > b} (T_i)_{ab} (T_j)_{ab}$,
so a root has $\dimension + \dimension (\dimension + 1) / 2$ rows,

$$
	C = \begin{pmatrix} W & -W & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & C_\theta \end{pmatrix},
	\qquad
	(C_\theta)_{(a, b),\, i} = \begin{cases} (T_i)_{aa} / \sqrt 2 & a = b, \\ (T_i)_{ab} & a > b, \end{cases} @tag(root)
$$

and $T_i$ in each form of the scale, with $E_{ab}$ the matrix unit and the diagonal entries
differentiated in their logarithmic charts, is

| form | coordinate | $T_i$ | $F$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| isotropic | $\log \sigma$ | $-2 I$ | $2 \dimension$ |
| diagonal | $\log \sigma_a$ | $-2 E_{aa}$ | $2 I$ |
| covariance factor | $L_{ab}$, $a > b$; $\log L_{aa}$ | $-(G + G\transpose)$, $G = W E_{ab}$; times $L_{aa}$ on the diagonal | $\frac12 \operatorname{tr}(T_i T_j)$ |
| whitening | $W_{ab}$, $a > b$; $\log W_{aa}$ | $G + G\transpose$, $G = E_{ab} L$; times $W_{aa}$ on the diagonal | $\frac12 \operatorname{tr}(T_i T_j)$ |

The rows for the covariance factor follow from $\partial \Lambda = -\Lambda\, \partial \Sigma\,
\Lambda$ and $\partial \Sigma = \partial L\, L\transpose + L\, \partial L\transpose$, those for the
whitening from $\partial \Lambda = \partial W\transpose W + W\transpose\, \partial W$, and the
logarithmic chart multiplies a diagonal column by the entry, by the derivative of the inclusion of
a positive value, $\operatorname{diag}(\point)$. The root of a scale given as a triangular factor
therefore needs both $W$ and $L$, whichever of them the model gives: the other is computed once and
folded when the scale is fixed, and by a solve [](eq:forward-substitution) on every evaluation when
it is free. This root is what the normal binds as the root of its expected information, among the
roles of [The roles](#the-roles).

## The tangent normal

A measurement of a curved quantity is most naturally written as the quantity measured, moved by
noise: a rotation measured by a star tracker, a direction to a landmark measured by a camera, a pose
measured by a scan matcher. With $h$ the map from the state to what is measured,

$$
	z = h(\point) \oplus \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon \sim \mathcal N(0, \Sigma) \text{ on } \R^\dimension, @tag(measurement)
$$

and a model is cleaner when it holds $z$, observed when the measurement is, than when it holds the
residual $z \ominus h(\point)$. The direction $u \in S^2$ to a landmark $\ell \in \R^3$ seen from a
pose $(R, p)$, for one, has $h(R, p) = R\transpose (\ell - p) / \lVert \ell - p \rVert$, a map into
the sphere written in storage and projected as [](eq:archive/core/math/map-into) allows. The law of
$z$ is a normal pushed through the chart at $h(\point)$, and its density is the subject of
[the note on densities in tangent charts](../../math/chart-volume/), on which this section rests.

**The density.** Let $\manifold$ be a space with a reference measure $\nu$ and, at every $\mu$, a
chart with domain $U_\mu$, image $V_\mu$ and log-volume $\lambda_\mu$
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/chart-volume)). The *tangent normal* $\mathcal T(\mu, \Sigma)$
is the law of $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$, with $\varepsilon$ normal on $\R^\dimension$ with mean zero
and covariance $\Sigma$, and its term is

$$
	\logterm_{\mathcal T}(\point;\, \mu, W)
	= -\tfrac12 \bigl\lVert W (\point \ominus \mu) \bigr\rVert^2
	+ \sum_{i=1}^{\dimension} \log W_{ii}
	- \tfrac{\dimension}{2} \log 2\pi
	- \lambda_\mu(\point \ominus \mu) , @tag(tangent-term)
$$

with the scale given in any of the forms of [The scale](#the-scale) and read through its whitening
$W$, as the normal's is.

<Statement {...tangentDensity} />

<Proof>

The vector $W^{-1} \zeta$ is normal with mean zero and covariance $\Sigma$, and the logarithm of its
density at $\tangent$ is the first three terms of [](eq:tangent-term) at $\tangent = \point \ominus
\mu$, as in the proof of [%full](statement:density). The chart is global, so $W^{-1} \zeta \in
U_\mu$ always, and [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward) gives the density. On a
Euclidean factor with Lebesgue measure the chart is a translation and its log-volume is zero; on a
positive one its volume is $\mu e^\tangent = \mu \oplus \tangent$, by the example of the note, so on
a product $\lambda_\mu(\point \ominus \mu) = \sum_{i \in P} \log \point_i = J(\point)$, and
[](eq:tangent-term) is [](eq:term).

</Proof>

So the normal of the previous sections is the tangent normal on the flat-in-disguise spaces, and
the two terms are one formula: the Gaussian log-density of the tangent coordinate, less the chart's
log-volume there. What the flat-in-disguise spaces add is the identity [](eq:translation), on which
the exactness of [%full](statement:information) rests, and which no curved space has.

**What a space supplies.** Beyond its retraction and its inverse, the term needs three things of the
space of $\point$:

1. *A reference measure.* The core fixes Lebesgue measure on its own types and leaves curved types
   without one ([the core's appendix](../core/math.svx#appendix-the-probability-space-of-a-model)).
   For an embedded type, this document takes the measure its retraction normalizes,
   [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized): Haar measure on the groups and surface
   measure on the sphere, whatever the storage, so that a density on a type means the same whether
   its points are stored as matrices or as quaternions.
2. *The log-volume of its chart*, as an operation on Euclidean values of the tangent coordinate,
   and of the point's storage where, as on the positive orthant, the volume moves with the point;
   with derivatives to order two, and smooth as computed at $\tangent = 0$, as the core asks of a
   type's retraction.
3. *The domain of its chart*: whether the chart is global, and if it is not, its domain.

It also needs $\point \ominus \mu$ on the whole image $V_\mu$ of the chart, not only near $\mu$,
which is more than [%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded) asks of a type's inverse
retraction. The charts of [the note's table](../../math/chart-volume/#the-usual-charts) all have
it: the logarithm on the rotations by less than $\pi$, the Cayley inverse
[](eq:math/chart-volume/cayley-inverse) away from the half-turns, and the stereographic inverse
[](eq:archive/core/math/sphere) away from $-\mu$.

The log-volume could be computed for any embedded type from its retraction alone, by the area
formula of [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized), but that formula contains the Jacobian
of $R$ in its value, so the term's second derivatives would need the third derivatives of $R$, which
no operation of the core has. A type declares its log-volume instead, as a formula, and the area
formula is what a test checks the declaration against, by a first-order pass over $R$. For the
spaces skoro means to support, the formulas are short: $2$, $4$ and $6 \log
\operatorname{sinc}(\theta / 2)$ of the rotation's angle for $SO(3)$, $SE(3)$ and $SE_2(3)$ with
their exponential charts, [](eq:math/chart-volume/so3-exp); $-2 \log(1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 /
4)$ for the Cayley chart of the rotations and the stereographic chart of the sphere; and, for a
product of a rotation with positions and velocities in a fixed frame of the world, the rotation's
alone. Each is one scalar function of the residual that the Gaussian part already computes.

**Exponential charts.** The exponential charts of the groups are not global, and the term
[](eq:tangent-term) on them is the density of the noise's part within a half-turn: by
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/local-chart), $\exp \logterm_{\mathcal T}$ integrates to
$1 - p$, where $p$ is the mass of the noise beyond a half-turn, and the law of $\mu \oplus
\varepsilon$, which wraps, is within $p$ in total variation of the law the term describes.

<Statement {...wrapping} />

<Proof>

The rotation's coordinates $\varepsilon_\phi$ are normal with covariance $\Sigma_{\phi\phi} = S^2$,
$S$ its symmetric square root, so they have the law of $S \zeta'$ with $\zeta'$ standard normal on
$\R^3$, and $\lVert S \zeta' \rVert \le \sigma \lVert \zeta' \rVert$, with equality when
$S = \sigma I$. So $p \le \Pr(\lVert \zeta' \rVert \ge \pi / \sigma)$. The norm $\lVert \zeta'
\rVert$ has the $\chi$ distribution with three degrees of freedom, of density
$\sqrt{2/\pi}\, s^2 e^{-s^2/2}$, and integration by parts gives
$\int_r^\infty s^2 e^{-s^2/2} \, \dd s = r e^{-r^2/2} + \sqrt{\pi/2}\, \operatorname{erfc}(r /
\sqrt 2)$, which at $r = \pi / \sigma$ is the claim.

</Proof>

At a few scales of the rotation's noise:

| $\sigma$ (rad) | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| bound on $p$ | $5 \times 10^{-34}$ | $1.4 \times 10^{-8}$ | $5.5 \times 10^{-4}$ | $0.020$ | $0.22$ |

For a measurement or a motion whose rotation is known to a few tenths of a radian, the wrapped mass
is far below anything an estimate could detect, and the exponential chart's term is exact in all
but name. For a rotation known to a radian or worse, such as the prior on the heading of a platform
that has not yet been aligned, it is not: the term's normalizer is off by $\log(1 - p)$, a function
of $\Sigma$ that matters when $\Sigma$ is estimated, and a fraction $p$ of the draws comes from
outside the law the term describes. A space with a global chart, the Cayley chart of the rotations,
has no such defect at any scale. The exact density of the wrapped law, a sum over the preimages
$(\theta + 2\pi k)\, a$, $k \in \mathbb Z$, of a rotation by $\theta$ about $a$, is another way
out, at the cost of a truncated series in every evaluation.

**The chart is part of the model.** The scale is read in the coordinates of the chart at $\mu$, so
two retractions of one space make two tangent normals with the same $\mu$ and $\Sigma$, which
agree only to first order: the exponential and Cayley coordinates of a rotation by $\theta$ have
the lengths $\theta$ and $2 \tan(\theta/2) = \theta + \theta^3/12 + O(\theta^5)$. A space's
tangent normals are therefore part of what its retraction means, and changing the retraction of a
type changes every model with a tangent normal on it, a little at small dispersion and much at
large. On a group, the side on which the retraction perturbs the point is part of it too, in a way
that can be undone exactly. The exponential and Cayley maps satisfy
$\varphi(\operatorname{Ad}_g \tangent) = g\, \varphi(\tangent)\, g^{-1}$, so
$\mu\, \varphi(\tangent) = \varphi(\operatorname{Ad}_\mu \tangent)\, \mu$, and a tangent normal
with scale $\Sigma$ in the point's own frame is the tangent normal with
$\operatorname{Ad}_\mu \Sigma \operatorname{Ad}_\mu\transpose$ in a fixed frame of the world. Its
log-volume is the same function in both, since it depends on the rotation's angle alone, which
$\operatorname{Ad}_\mu$ preserves.

On the sphere there is a further dependence, on the basis $B(\mu)$ that identifies the tangent plane
with $\R^2$. No basis is smooth on all of $S^2$, so a field of bases turns as $\mu$ moves in a way
that the field, not the geometry, decides, and it spins near the point where it fails, the south
pole of the core's acceptance test. A scale that is not isotropic is read along the basis's axes,
and so means something different at every $\mu$. An isotropic scale is free of the basis: with an
orthonormal basis, the stereographic chart's $\lVert \point \ominus \mu \rVert = 2 \tan(\alpha/2)$
and the normalization chart's $\tan \alpha$ depend only on the angle $\alpha$ between $\point$ and
$\mu$.

**What the mean is.** The point $\mu$ is the centre of the chart in which the noise is normal. It is
not the expectation, which on an embedded type lies off the space; on the positive orthant it is
the median, as [The density](#the-density) says; and on a curved space it need not even be a mode.
On $SO(3)$ with the exponential chart and an isotropic scale $\sigma$, the density along a ray from
$\mu$ is proportional to $\exp(-\theta^2 / 2\sigma^2 - \lambda(\theta))$, whose logarithm has the
second derivative $-1/\sigma^2 + 1/6$ at $\theta = 0$ by [](eq:math/chart-volume/so3-exp), so
$\mu$ is a mode only when $\sigma^2 < 6$. With the Cayley chart, $-\lambda = 2 \log(1 + \lVert
\tangent \rVert^2 / 4)$ has the second derivative $1$ at $0$, and $\mu$ is a mode only when
$\sigma < 1$ in its coordinates: a broad normal in Cayley coordinates piles its mass near the
half-turns, where the chart compresses most.

**The estimate a measurement gives.** When $z$ is observed and the state $\point$ is free, the term
[](eq:tangent-term) at $\mu = h(\point)$ depends on $\point$ through the whitened residual and
through $\lambda_{h(\point)}(z \ominus h(\point))$. Near a zero residual, $-\lambda$ is
$\lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 12$ for the exponential chart of the rotations and
$\lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 2$ for the Cayley and stereographic charts, to second order, so the
measurement weighs on the state as a Gaussian of the residual would with the precision $\Lambda$
replaced by $\Lambda - \tfrac16 I$ or $\Lambda - I$ on the rotation's block. For a rotation's
noise of $0.1$ rad that changes the measurement's weight by $0.17\,\%$ and $1\,\%$. Small, but it is
the difference between the law [](eq:measurement) and the law that least squares of the whitened
residual estimates exactly, which the paragraph *Without a volume* below describes.

**Derivatives.** Every step of [](eq:tangent-term) is an operation the core differentiates: the
inverse retraction through the boundary operations of [%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded),
the whitening as in [The scale](#the-scale), and the log-volume as the type's declared operation.
The term's order is the least of theirs, two when the type's inverse retraction and log-volume
admit Hessian passes.

**The sampler.** The tangent normal draws $\mu \oplus L \zeta$ from the variates of the quantity's
key, as the normal does ([The sampler](#the-sampler)). On a global chart the draw is exact for the
term by [%full](statement:tangent-density), and contributes nothing to the importance weight. On an
exponential chart it draws the wrapped law, within $p$ of the term's in total variation, so an
expectation of a function bounded by $1$ that importance weighting estimates is off by at most
$2p$.

**The expected information.** Of [%full](statement:information), only the scale's block survives
exactly, and only on a global chart. There the residual of a draw is exactly its noise,
$(\mu \oplus \varepsilon) \ominus \mu = \varepsilon$ for every $\varepsilon$, of covariance
$\Sigma$; neither $\mu$ nor $\lambda$ depends on the scale's coordinates $\theta$; and the
computation of $F$ in that proof holds unchanged. The blocks in $\point$ and $\mu$ involve the
second derivatives of $\ominus$ and of $\lambda$, which [](eq:translation) made constant and which
no curved space has constant. The mixed blocks between $\mu$ and $\theta$ need not vanish either.
On $SO(3)$ with the exponential chart, the derivative of $\varepsilon = \point \ominus \mu$ along
$\mu \operatorname{Exp}(s u)$ is $-J_l(\varepsilon)^{-1} u = (-I + \tfrac12 [\varepsilon]_\times -
c(\theta) [\varepsilon]_\times^2)\, u$, with $c$ even in $\varepsilon$, so the expected mixed second
derivative of $-\logterm_{\mathcal T}$ along $u$ and $\theta_i$ is
$-\tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}(\Sigma\, \partial_i \Lambda\, [u]_\times)$, up to the wrapped mass. It
is zero for every $u$ only when $\Sigma\, \partial_i \Lambda$ is symmetric, as for an isotropic or a
diagonal scale.

The tangent normal's root, which [The roles](#the-roles) binds as the normal's is bound, is
therefore [](eq:root) unchanged: $\pm W$ in the blocks of $\point$ and $\mu$, which are the
derivatives of $\point \ominus \mu$ at a zero residual, and $C_\theta$ in the scale's. On a
curved space its accuracy is that of each block. In the scale's block it is exact on a global
chart. In the blocks of $\point$ and $\mu$ it is exact at a zero residual, and its relative error
is of second order in the dispersion: the derivatives of $\ominus$ differ from $\pm I$ by terms
of first order in $\varepsilon$, whose expectation in a quadratic form is of second order since
$\varepsilon$ is symmetric; the second derivatives of $\ominus$ meet the residual $\Lambda
\varepsilon$, whose expectation is zero; and the Hessian of $\lambda$, $\tfrac16 I$ on the
rotations with the exponential chart, is of order $\Sigma$ against $\Lambda$. The mixed block,
which the root sets to zero, is of first order, the correlation it omits between the mean and the
scale being of the order of the dispersion; on $SO(3)$ with the exponential chart it is zero for
the isotropic and diagonal forms. This is the archive's information, with its error now stated.

**Without a volume.** A space that declares no reference measure or no log-volume can still carry a
measurement, as a likelihood factor. With $z$ a constant, the measured value, the Gaussian part
$-\tfrac12 \lVert W (z \ominus h(\point)) \rVert^2$ is a function of $\point$ that scores nothing,
and as a likelihood factor it needs no measure. It is not the log-likelihood of the law
[](eq:measurement), from which it lacks $\lambda$, a function of $\point$. It is, exactly, the
log-likelihood of another law.

<Statement {...invariant} />

<Proof>

Substitute $z = \mu g$. The measure $\nu$ is invariant under left translation, $V_\mu = \mu V_e$,
and $z \ominus \mu = \varphi^{-1}(\mu^{-1} z) = g \ominus e$, so $Z(\mu) = Z(e)$. The second form
is [](eq:math/chart-volume/image) at $e$, with $g(\tangent) = \exp(-\tfrac12 \lVert W \tangent
\rVert^2)$.

</Proof>

So on a group whose chart translates one of the identity, $\exp(-\tfrac12 \lVert W (z \ominus \mu)
\rVert^2) / Z$ is a density of $z$ with respect to Haar measure for every $\mu$, its log-likelihood
in $\mu$ is the Gaussian part up to a constant, and least squares of the whitened residual is
exact maximum likelihood for it. This is the model that factor-graph libraries estimate. Its $z$ is
not $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$ for a normal $\varepsilon$, so it has no sampler of that form, and $Z$
has no closed form, so the factor is a likelihood only for a fixed scale. On the sphere the same
holds for an isotropic scale with the stereographic or the normalization chart, whose residual's
length depends only on the angle to $\mu$. A space without a volume therefore carries a measurement
as a likelihood factor over data, for estimating a state with a known scale, and a space with one
carries the tangent normal, whose measurement is a quantity like any other.

## As a likelihood factor

A retirement's replacement is a likelihood factor over the quantities of its blanket $B$, scoring
none of them ([the core's retirement](../core/math.svx#retirement)). Under a Laplace approximation,
and exactly for linear-Gaussian terms, it is a Gaussian function of their tangent coordinates at an
anchor: with constants $p_i$ for the blanket's quantities, the stacked tangent

$$
	\delta(\point_B) = (\point_1 \ominus p_1;\ \dots;\ \point_k \ominus p_k) \in \R^D,
	\qquad D = \sum_i \dimension_i, @tag(stacked)
$$

and

$$
	\logterm_B(\point_B) = \logterm_{\mathcal N}\bigl(\delta(\point_B);\, m, W\bigr) + c , @tag(joint)
$$

the normal's term on $\R^D$, where $J$ vanishes, at the mapped value $\delta$, with mean $m$,
whitening $W$ and a constant $c$. No new distribution is needed for it: it is the normal on a
Euclidean space evaluated at a value that is not a quantity, which scores nothing and so has no
sampler. The archive wrote its residual as $W\delta - b$, an offset form; that is [](eq:joint) with
$b = W m$, which the core folds when both are constants. The anchors must be constants, so that
the factor does not move with the quantities it summarizes. On a positive blanket quantity $\delta$
reads its logarithm, and no Jacobian is added: a likelihood factor is a function of the quantities,
not a density over them, and whatever measure the approximated integral was taken in is already in
its values. The same holds of a curved blanket quantity: $\delta$ reads its tangent coordinate at
its anchor, and no log-volume is added.

The core's tests of retirement are of this form. The replacement
[](eq:archive/core/math/kalman) is [](eq:joint) with $k = 1$, the anchor $p_1 = a + K(\hat z_0 -
a)$ at that equation's updated mean, $m = 0$,
$W = (P + q^2)^{-1/2}$ and $c = \log \mathcal N(a, s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0)$, and
[](eq:archive/core/math/joint-blanket) is [](eq:joint) with $k = 2$, both anchors at $a$, $m = 0$,
$c = 0$, and $W$ the whitening of its $2 \times 2$ covariance. With the normal in the library,
both can be written with it rather than by hand.

## The roles

The charter has a distribution declare, as its term is added, the nodes that readers other than the
model's density need, each under a *role*, and has each role arrive with the distribution or reader
that gives it meaning ([position 8](../charter/writeup.md)). No reader of the normal's roles exists
yet: the least-squares readers and Fisher scoring are entries of their own. The normal binds them
all the same, so that those readers find them waiting rather than revising this entry, and so that
the mathematics of each binding, which is the normal's to settle, is settled here.

| role | multiplicity | the node it binds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| whitened residual | exactly one | $r = W (\point \ominus \mu) \in \R^\dimension$, computed as the form of the scale computes it |
| mean | exactly one | $\mu$ |
| scale | exactly one | the scale as the model gives it, $\sigma$, $\sigma$, $L$ or $W$, together with its form |
| log-volume | at most one | $\lambda_\mu(\point \ominus \mu)$, where it is not identically zero |
| information root | at most one | the root $C$ of [](eq:root) |

The charter's example binds a covariance factor. With four forms of the scale, the role binds
whichever the model gave, and its form, from which a reader computes $W$ or $L$ as
[The scale](#the-scale) says; in the covariance factor's form the residual is the solution $z$ of
[](eq:forward-substitution), which is $W (\point \ominus \mu)$ without $W$ ever being formed.

**What a reader of the residual estimates.** The term is $-\tfrac12 \lVert r \rVert^2$ plus a
normalizer in the scale, less the log-volume. A least-squares reader that minimizes
$\tfrac12 \lVert r \rVert^2$ over the free quantities, with the scale fixed, therefore maximizes the
term itself only where the log-volume is constant in them. On a Euclidean space it is zero. On a
positive factor it is $J(\point)$, constant when $\point$ is observed, and not when $\point$ is
free, where ignoring it finds the mode in the logarithmic chart rather than with respect to Lebesgue
measure. On a curved space it is $\lambda_\mu(\point \ominus \mu)$, which moves with the mean, so
that a measurement's estimate of the state differs by the precision change of
[The tangent normal](#the-tangent-normal). The log-volume's role is what lets a reader add its
gradient and Hessian, and so estimate the tangent normal's own mode; a reader that ignores it
estimates, on a group, the law of [%full](statement:invariant), the model of factor-graph
libraries, and should say so.

**The information root.** The role binds the matrix $C$ of [](eq:root), of
$\dimension + \dimension(\dimension+1)/2$ rows, whose three block columns a reader composes with
the Jacobians, with respect to the free quantities, of $\point$, of $\mu$ and of the scale's
coordinates $\theta$. When the scale is a constant there is no $\theta$, and the root is its first
block row, $(W \;\, -W)$. On the flat-in-disguise spaces the root is exact by
[%full](statement:information); on a curved space its accuracy, block by block, is the one
[The tangent normal](#the-tangent-normal) states.

**Likelihood factors.** A likelihood factor in the forms of this document, a retirement's
replacement [](eq:joint) or a measurement over data on a space without a volume, binds the whitened
residual, the mean and the scale, and binds no information root: the expected information is an
expectation over the quantity a term scores, and a likelihood factor scores none. A reader that needs
a positive semidefinite matrix from one has it in the Gauss–Newton product of its residual's
Jacobian, which the residual's role gives, and which for the replacement [](eq:joint), a Gaussian
of $\delta$, is its negative Hessian in $\delta$.

## In skoro's terms

- **The normal scores one quantity** $\point$, of a flat-in-disguise type: real, vector, matrix,
  positive, or a product of them. Its term [](eq:term) is a composite of the core's primitives,
  conditioned on the quantities its mean and scale are reached from, which may be constants,
  quantities or mapped values of any kind the core allows. Its log-density is with respect to the
  core's base measure, Jacobian [](eq:jacobian) included.
- **Its derivatives** with respect to $\point$, to $\mu$ and to the scale, of first and second
  order, are those of the composite. Every step of it, from the inverse retraction to the
  logarithms of the diagonal and the substitution [](eq:forward-substitution), is a primitive of
  order two, so the term has order two.
- **Its scale** is given in any of the four forms of [The scale](#the-scale), all of which it
  offers: positive values for
  the isotropic and diagonal forms, and the product $\positive^\dimension \times
  \R^{\dimension(\dimension-1)/2}$ for a triangular factor when it is a quantity. A fixed scale's
  whitening and normalizer are folded, in the model when it is a constant and in a compiled query
  when it is observed.
- **Its sampler is exact**, drawing $\mu \oplus L \Phi^{-1}(u)$ from the variates of the
  quantity's key, on the host in double precision.
- **Its expected information** over $(\point, \mu, \theta)$ is exact, with the root [](eq:root).
- **Its roles**, bound as its term is added though no reader uses them yet, are its whitened
  residual, its mean, its scale with its form, its log-volume where it is not zero, and the root
  [](eq:root) of its expected information ([The roles](#the-roles)).
- **As a likelihood factor**, the normal on $\R^D$ evaluated at the stacked tangent [](eq:stacked)
  of a blanket at constant anchors is the form of a retirement's Gaussian replacement.
- **The tangent normal scores one quantity** of any continuous type whose space has a reference
  measure and declares its chart's log-volume and domain. Its term [](eq:tangent-term) is
  conditioned on the quantities its mean and scale are reached from; its mean is any value of the
  quantity's type, a mapped value $h(\point)$ of the state among them. On the flat-in-disguise
  spaces it is the normal.
- **A measurement** [](eq:measurement) is such a quantity, held as what was measured rather than as
  a residual: observed in a query that has the measurement, and free, to be drawn, in one that
  simulates it.
- **Its sampler** draws $\mu \oplus L \Phi^{-1}(u)$, exact on a global chart and within the
  wrapped mass of [%full](statement:wrapping) on an exponential one. It binds the normal's roles,
  its root [](eq:root) among them, which on a curved space is exact in the scale's block on a
  global chart, of second order in the blocks of $\point$ and $\mu$, and of first order in the
  mixed block it omits.
- **A space without a volume** carries a measurement as a likelihood factor over data, the
  Gaussian part of [](eq:tangent-term) at a constant measured value, which with a fixed scale is
  the exact log-likelihood of the law of [%full](statement:invariant).

